# REPAIR REPORT r1 — Book 7, Movement 1 "Unmeasured" (chapters 1–7)

Author: `claude-opus-5-5` (Claude Opus 5.5), O'Connor 1.3.0 seat, same author as the draft, 2026-10-11. Brief: `REPAIR-BRIEF.md`. Baseline for every "before": `state/movement-001/pre-repair-r1/` (identical to the manuscript when I began, the coordinator's two audit fixes included). Chapters 5 and 7 were re-read in full from disk before any edit.

No file under `books/` was opened, read or searched. No git command was run. Nothing was written outside `manuscript/chapter-01.md`–`chapter-07.md`, this report, and the state section of `AUTHOR-REPORT.md`. Every change was an individually written replacement of an exact passage; no rule or script split or joined sentences.

**Result:** 37,126 words (was 36,924; the brief expects 36,950 ± 500) · gates 0/0/0 · overlap 0 unprotected, 17 protected · sentence mean 14.03 · SD 11.79 (was 11.62) · ≥40 words 4.3% · ≤5 words 28.7% · 977 words per scene. Twenty-seven protected and packet units were re-checked after the edits; each stands exactly once and unchanged.

## The coordinator's two fixes — kept, joins smoothed

- **ch5:235, the buckle.** The opening sentence "*It tracked the buckle.*" is untouched and no sense is named. Before: "That was worth having, though not for itself. The buckle moved at forty-five yards and the grey head moved with it. A wired wheel, ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth had drawn no answer Cael could see. The observation told him neither what sense had found the buckle nor what the thing was waiting for." After: "That was worth having, and it was all that he had. At forty-five yards the grey head had moved when the buckle moved, and had lost it when the buckle stopped; a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth had drawn no answer that Cael could see. He could not say what sense had found the buckle, and the finding told him nothing of what the thing was waiting for."
- **ch5:201, one other line checked for sense.** "…when taking out a pencil might be a thing this country heard." → "…might, for all she knew, be a declaration too." Nothing else in chapters 5–7 states how the animal perceives. Seln's "waiting upon a signal" and Cael's "waiting to be told something" stand, as ruled.
- **ch7:21–23, Pike's Thornwater line.** The coordinator's sentence is kept word for word and now closes the paragraph that sets it up: "Pike nodded, and turned his shoulder to the meadow, and raised his level, carrying voice over the heads of the people at the plank, so that it went as far as the ash. The Thornwater line went out exactly as it had come to them on bark." The next paragraph no longer repeats "on a scrap of bark" ("Cael had read those words in a hold's yard, and thought them his"). The document is not re-quoted.

## A. One distance (editorial 1)

Canon kept: **a hundred and ten yards** from gate to stone; **forty-five yards** of following distance. Each person's count now appears once, with its relation to the yards.

| Where | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| ch5:255 | "Then he counted his own strides from abreast of it to the gate, as Karis would have counted, with his lips shut." (then "A hundred and ten.") | "Then he lengthened his stride to the yard, as he had learned to do on sixty feet of oak, and counted from abreast of it to the gate, as Karis would have counted, with his lips shut." (then "A hundred and ten.") So his count is yards by construction. |
| ch6:9 | "a hundred and ten yards off beside a white stone" | unchanged; now agrees |
| ch6:109 | "the figure, *110*, and after it, in smaller writing, *of C.'s strides, gate to stone.* She had gone out to the well in the heat of noon and paced a hundred and ten of her own along the inside of the fence, which came to a good deal less…" | "the figure, *110 yards*, and after it, in smaller writing, *gate to stone; C.'s count, paced at a yard to the stride; not taped.* She had gone out in the heat of noon and walked the same hundred and ten yards along the inside of the fence, in her own shorter steps, so as to know in her legs how far it was." (No stride count is given for Karis, so nothing here can collide with her cord-and-stride figures in Movement 4.) |
| ch6:123 | "It was a hundred and four of his paces; the boy's stride was a little shorter than his own." | "The boy had made it a hundred and ten yards from the gate, at a yard to the stride, and Seln's own longer pace brought him there in a hundred and four, which was the same distance to within a yard or two." |
| ch6:137 | Seln's return: "Halted one hundred and ten yards from the hold's gate" | unchanged |
| ch6:145 | "He had now eight roads in his head, and the manner of an animal's waiting." | "He had now the first hours of eight roads in his head, five out of Lowmarch and three out of this fence, and the manner of an animal's waiting." |
| ch7:261 | Karis: "There are five roads off that edge… I don't know where one of them ends." | "There are five roads out of that ring… Three of them run off the hide before they come to anything." Seln's reply ("I have walked the first part of every one") is unchanged and now plainly means Lowmarch's five. |

Karis still measures by writing the provenance of a figure; Seln still measures in his own paces and checks them against somebody else's yards.

## B. "their four" (editorial 2)

ch7:323 (was 321). Before: "He had heard their four said, or heard of them. A boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon." After: "He had heard their four lines said at the plank, or heard of them: a boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon." Nothing else in Teague's assessment changed.

## C. Whose deployment the line counts (cold 1)

ch1:5. Before: "He had written that under the inventory an hour ago, and since then he had done nothing at all but keep it true." After: "He had written that under the inventory an hour ago. The last line of it was his own count and nobody else's: it was for the nine things he carried, and whether he had let one of them out. Since then he had done nothing at all but keep it true." One clarification, at the first entry. Every later repetition is as terse as it was, and no power event changed.

## D. A firmer handhold in the opening (cold 2)

Three paragraphs of ch1, a clause or a sentence each. No glossary and no account of the hill.

- ch1:9. "It was an old habit, and he had it from a hill." → "It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him."
- ch1:29. "He had no Path, and so no tier and no rank… An Arbiter had woken in him at fourteen… pieces of other people's Paths, each one taken inside a fight…" → "He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank… Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price." (Never "his Path"; his own Arbiter woke and went dark.)
- ch1:35. "None of that was use, and he had used nothing since a ring of churned turf beside a split thorn." → "None of that was use. He had spent two years keeping such things down under other people's eyes, and he had let none of them out since a ring of churned turf beside a split thorn, from a caution that he could not yet have given a reason for."

## E. The nine-fragment rent inventory (cold 3)

ch4:81–113. The entry and its frame went from 629 words to 534; the scene from 913 to 817. All nine are kept, with every limit the stretch needs: Wind's one-count landing lock; Pressure's visible gathering over a breath; the Iron read's pace and a half; Compression at contact; Ember's one point and one channel; Shadow sealed, costing from waking, drifting at dusk; Storm's air-or-body rule and the three lane bills (ten, ten, twelve; clean at eleven, eleven, thirteen; a bill, not a flag; untried past three); Anchor's points, lines and cage, its one purposeful unbinding, its ten/twelve/fourteen feet, its unknown reach, rate and price, and its doorways; Blade's *Edge?*. The anomaly and its three words are unchanged.

- **Framing tightened.** The paragraph before the entry said the rent idea, and the entry's first line said it again. Now the lead-in is "…and it took him the better part of an hour. On the hill he had only ever listed what each of the nine could do." and the entry opens "*The nine, as things held. Not what each can do: what it charges me to carry it and leave it alone, which I'm calling rent.*" The word "Rent:" no longer heads four of the items.
- **One source-history detail moved to where it is active.** Anchor's uncommanded twelve-second doorway hold left the inventory and went to the ladder at Thornwater, where Anchor offers to hold a rail without being asked (ch6:75: "It had held a doorway once, for twelve seconds, without waiting for his leave, and he answered it quickly.").
- **Three cross-references cut** as restatement of scenes already on the page: Pressure "tried at the tree on the river"; "Karis lasted ninety today"; Blade "was right once, on a scree".
- Lira's reply follows the new text: "You've written *rent* seven times" / "Six. Compression doesn't charge, and Blade only asks." → "That's a bill against every one of them" / "Eight. Compression charges nothing until I'm hit." Her next line ("It's a good deal of rent, for a man with no income.") is unchanged.

The hold drills are untouched.

## F. The tracked passage — chain broken (source distance)

ch7:27–43 (was ch7:29–41). I did not open the source; I worked from the chain as the brief describes it.

Before, in order: a woman identified with exactly three followers at her back → she lays a slip on the plank without speaking → Pike reads it → her objection → the witness.

After, in order: Pike is already reading. The slip "lay ready under the edge of his stone, where somebody had left it for him earlier in the day", and he gives it to the meadow before the five's silver is off the oak. → "Lame when I took him." comes from behind Cael's shoulder, from somebody he "had taken for one of the crowd"; she is described there, alone. → "Which foot?" and the witness, as before. → Only after the witness nods do "two men and a girl" get up off the bank and come to stand at her back: "Cael saw that it was a captain who had spoken, and that her crew had left her to say it alone until it was proved." → Pike writes, and reads it after.

She does not present the slip, she is not introduced through her followers, and the first thing in the passage is Pike's voice. The plan's requirement (a woman captain's line read before and after, with a correction between) is intact, and the two protected Pike lines that follow are untouched.

## G. Sentence range in reflective narration (editorial 3)

About forty joins by reading, all in unprotected reflective or expository narration: the road and board explanations, the two Seln windows, Teague's window, and Cael's reading of him. Dialogue, log entries, protected text, scene breaks and the short landing beats were left alone ("Nobody was there."; "It lifted."; "They went in."; "It had done nothing. It had answered nothing."; "Four."; "A hundred and ten."). After the joins the long-sentence share stood at 4.5%, so eight long action sentences elsewhere were cut back by hand to keep it at 4.3%.

Representative joins (chapter:line after):
- ch1:335 "He did not know why, and that was the weakness in the ruling, which he looked at squarely, for not one of the five things said what the rule was a rule against."
- ch2:17 "He looked for the wall first, since every town he had ever entered had kept one, even Denvash, which was nothing; and a wall meant a gate, and a gate meant a man in it with a book."
- ch2:129 "Not one of the fourteen said who might take it: none of them asked for a tier, or a rank, or a Path of any kind…"
- ch4:255 "…he did the three things that he had always done: he found the place where a man would stand who wished to watch the road without being seen from it, and the place where the road was narrowest, and he looked for water, and paced it from where a camp might lie."
- ch5:49 "Cael did what he always did, and began with the claim, which was a mile of road that carters crossed only at one hour of the day."
- ch5:95 "He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too."
- ch6:127 "A creature that stands a moment leaves a mark like a seal in wax, and one that stands until its weight has settled forward leaves a mark like these; he judged that it had been there from the time a gate opened until the time an arrow went back into its case."
- ch6:229 "Each of them had opened on a day that somebody else appointed, and each had closed with a paper."
- ch7:63 "People had read them about him in rooms he was not let into, and he had stood on a carved number for fourteen sittings and never once been shown the page."
- ch7:321 "He did not read the top of it, since he knew what was at the top; he read the bottom, where the ink was new…"
- ch7:377 "It was not the registry's look, which he knew in his sleep… This look stopped at what was there: it took his hands, and the book under them, and how he sat."

A few word choices went with them (ch3:213 "what looked at him was a river"; ch4:273 "he had given them the gauge of the east road"). I stopped there. The spread widened a little (SD 11.62 → 11.79) and the mean rose from 13.70 to 14.03, inside the brief's hold of 13.3–14.2.

