| Keep reading | 8/10 | 9/10 | Chapter 5 lines 167–259, the moving hole in the frogs through the opened gate; Chapter 7’s final mutual reading then opens the Teague pressure cleanly. | High |
| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | 9/10 | Chapter 2 line 131 (“It doesn’t price us”) and line 215 (the missing sixth column): progression is reframed through labor, reputation, and recordkeeping rather than a familiar rank ladder. | High |
| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | 8/10 | The wash and stretch are spatially legible, but Chapter 6 lines 9/109/123/137 conflict on distance units and Chapter 7 line 321 obscures its referent. | High |
| Character attachment | 8/10 | 9/10 | Chapter 3 lines 99, 217, and 273 give the horse a fitting end, let Cael find rest in being merely visible, and give Brom his first earned money. Chapter 4 line 127 turns Cael’s old punishment into useful teaching. | High |
| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | 8/10 | Chapter 1 line 85 (“red not-pine”), Chapter 4 line 237 (the bread and the smith), and Chapter 6 line 241 (geese like a jury) vary the humor by character and never dismiss fear or pain. | High |
