    93	"They do, with me." Brom stood quite still under it. "I've never known why. I think it's that I'm the same height as a post."
    94	
    95	Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
    96	
    97	They camped cold, in a hollow, a good way back from a stream. Lira and Seln took the first watch and Brom and Karis the second. It was Cael's night to be the fifth, which he spent lying on his back, hearing every sound the scrub made and failing to sleep through any of them.
    98	
    99	The second day went well until two hours after noon.
   100	
   101	They had forded the valley's water twice by then, at shallow places with no name, in good light, and the carter had begun to walk with his shoulders down. Then the off wheel gave a crack like a stick breaking. It ran on three turns more, wobbling, and came off its axle altogether, and the cart sat down on that corner with a jolt that sent Karis's hand to the canvas at last.
   102	
   103	The pin had sheared. That was the least of it, for the wheel had dropped onto a stone as it went, and one of the curved timbers of its rim had split along the grain for half its length.
   104	
   105	Brom looked at it. Then he looked at the carter, who was looking at the sun.
   106	
   107	"How long?" said the carter.
   108	
   109	"I don't know yet. Have you wire?"
   110	
   111	He had wire. They got a pole under the axle, and Brom lifted, with the cords standing in his neck and no grey in him anywhere, while Seln and Cael built stones up under the bed. He whittled a new pin out of thorn with the hedging bill, and bound the split rim round and round, and twisted the wire tight with the back of the blade. Karis timed him, since nobody had told her not to.
   112	
   113	"An hour and four minutes," she said, when the wheel was on. "By the only clock there is."
   114	
   115	The carter said nothing at all. He took the mule's head, and they went on faster than before, and the wired wheel ticked at every turn.
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	They came to the mouth of it with the sun a hand's breadth above the ridge behind them.
   120	
   121	Cael knew the place before anybody named it. The scrub ended, as though it had been cut with a knife, and the valley drew in between two long shoulders of hill, and from one shoulder to the other the whole floor was grass. It was the pale grass of the lower river, chest high, and with the light coming level along it every blade stood up gold on one side and grey on the other. The ruts went into it and were lost within thirty yards.
   122	
   123	The carter had stopped the mule. He stood with his fist in its cheek-strap and looked up the valley, and he spoke for the second time that day.
   124	
   125	"That's it. A mile, near enough. The fence stands across the head of it, and you can't see that from here."
   126	
   127	"We could camp," said Brom. "Short of it. And go through at noon."
   128	
   129	"Nobody camps at the mouth."
   130	
   131	"Why not?"
   132	
   133	"A man did," said the carter.
   134	
   135	He seemed to feel that he had explained the matter, and after a moment Cael found that he agreed.
   136	
   137	Seln had been looking at the grass, in sections, from the near ground outward. He turned round, and for once he did not wait for anybody to look at him first.
   138	
   139	"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
   140	
   141	"Behind," said Brom.
   142	
   143	"A thing that follows a cart comes up on its tail. I should like it to find you there."
   144	
   145	"And what do we do, if it does?" said Lira.
   146	
   147	"What he has paid for. We walk." Seln looked at the carter's fist on the strap. "Everything you carry that has an edge, carry it where it shows. Nothing else. I mean that as exactly as I have ever meant anything."
   148	
   149	They went in.
   150	
   151	For the first quarter of a mile there was nothing but the noise, and Cael had not known until then how much noise a quiet valley makes. There were frogs. The bottom was wet, somewhere under the grass, and they were in it by the hundred, a creaking that rose and fell like something breathing. There were crickets, nearer, in a dry steady saw, and some small bird that ticked. Over all of it the wind went through a mile of standing grass with a sound like a hand drawn slowly down a coat.
   152	
   153	The cart had its own noises, and he learned them, since they were the only ones he could do nothing about. The wired wheel ticked. The grain shifted in its sacks. And on the mule's harness, somewhere behind the saddle-pad, a loose buckle clicked against its own tongue at every step.
   154	
   155	He walked in the left rut with his knife on his belt and looked outward, as he had been told. The sun touched the ridge behind him. His shadow went out in front, forty feet long, and Lira's beside it, and then quite suddenly there were no shadows.
   156	
   157	A little way past that, on the left, something stood up out of the grass that was not grass. It was a cart-shaft. There was only the one, grey and split, pointing at the sky at the angle of a man's raised arm, and whatever it had once been fastened to lay down in the stems where nobody could see it. The carter did not turn his head as they went by. Karis did, and then looked very steadily at the mule's ears.
   158	
   159	Lira put her hand up.
   160	
   161	She did not stop walking. She only raised her right hand to the height of her shoulder, with the fingers shut, and held it there. She had spent her life listening to moving air and knew its sounds better than a carter knows his mule.
   162	
   163	Cael listened where she was looking.
   164	
   165	It took him a dozen strides to hear it, because it was a thing that was not there. Out on the right, forty or fifty yards into the grass, the frogs had stopped. They had not stopped everywhere. They had stopped in one place, a patch no wider than a room, and all round the edge of that place they creaked as before. It was a hole in the noise.
   166	
   167	The hole was moving. It went along beside them through the bottom, up the valley, at exactly the pace of a mule.
   168	
   169	"Don't declare anything," said Seln.
   170	
   171	He had not turned his head. He was thirty paces in front, a dark coat walking the verge, and his voice came back along the ruts as small and plain as a pebble skimmed on water.
   172	
   173	"Walk. Same pace."
   174	
   175	He meant a Path. He meant Lira's burst, and Brom's Iron Skin, and any point that Karis might set; and Cael knew, with a coldness in his stomach, that he meant nine fragments as well, which were nobody's Path at all.
   176	
   177	---
   178	
   179	It showed itself at the half mile.
   180	
   181	The ground rose a little there, on the right, in a low stony swell where the grass grew thin; and something came up out of the tall stuff onto that rise and went along the top of it, where the last of the light lay.
   182	
   183	It was grey. That was the first thing, and for some strides it was the only one, for the grey was the grey of the grass in shadow and the eye kept sliding off. Then it moved against the sky, and he had the shape of it. It was low, and long in the back, and about as high at the shoulder as a calf. It carried its head level and stretched out, like a dog on a scent. It went at a walk, and not one stem of grass moved round its legs.
   184	
   185	He measured the distance, since he had no other use for his eyes. He had stood on sixty feet of sprung oak often enough to lay that length down anywhere, and he laid it end to end across the bottom, from his own boot to the rise. Two lengths and a quarter. Forty-five yards.
   186	
   187	"I could clear it," said Lira.
   188	
   189	She barely breathed it. She was in the right-hand rut, a yard from him, looking straight in front of her, and every line of her was strung as it had been on the gravel three strides short of a mark.
   190	
   191	"No," said Seln.
   192	
   193	"I know. I'm saying it so I've said it."
   194	
   195	She walked on. Her hands were open at her sides, and he saw her set her feet down one after the other with a care that had nothing to do with the ground.
   196	
   197	Ten strides later a stone turned under her heel. It was nothing, a loose cobble in the rut, such as any of them had trodden on forty times that day; but her whole weight went sideways, and in the instant before she had her balance he felt the air beside him tighten, as it tightens in a yard before somebody goes. Her hand shot out and shut on his sleeve. She did not go. She hung there for one stride with her fingers twisted in his coat and her teeth showing, and then she had her foot under her, and let go of him, and smoothed the cloth down where she had gripped it as though that had been all she meant to do.
   198	
   199	He looked back once, across his shoulder, because he could not help it.
   200	
   201	Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might, for all she knew, be a declaration too. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
   202	
   203	Behind her the carter walked with his palm spread over the mule's nostrils, and the mule's ears lay back along its neck.
   204	
   205	And behind the tailboard came Brom.
   206	
   207	Cael had seen him fill a doorway and had seen him fill a district's frame. He had seen him make a harness room feel small by standing up in it. He had never seen this. Brom was walking with the bill held low across his thighs, and his arms were brown to the wrist, and somehow he had drawn in everything about himself that was more than bone and muscle and a coat. He was exactly as large as his body and no larger. It was still a good deal of body, and it was all there was, and it walked at the pace of a mule and looked at nothing.
   208	
   209	Cael faced front again, and all nine of them were waiting for him.
   210	
   211	Wind had his feet first. They had leaned for a month, a dozen times a day, and they leaned now toward the rise as if the ground sloped that way. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, slowly, a breath at a time. The Iron read wanted to open and tell him the mass of everything within a pace and a half, which was nothing but a mule; it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. Ember offered a point in the dry grass at the foot of the swell, and behind it another and another, so that he could see how the whole slope would take fire along a line. Anchor had found the wired rim and asked about it. Blade wanted Brom's bill. Storm turned over, once, like a great fish under a boat, and showed him the air between himself and the grey thing as a lane already laid.
   212	
   213	And it was dusk. Shadow came up behind all the rest, soft, the oldest of his refusals, with the only offer that he had ever truly wanted to take. It could make less of him. He could go up that valley and not be there at all.
   214	
   215	*No*, he told it, and Wind, and Pressure. *No. Rim; his; leave it. No.* He said it to each of them at one stride and to each of them again at the next. They were small refusals, one after another, quicker than he could have put names to them, and none of them was any harder than lowering a hand; it was only that there were nine hands, and that they rose again as often as he put them down.
   216	
   217	He had done this for two years so that a house on a bluff might keep its charter, and he had hated every Fifth-day of it. He was doing it now so that a mule, and a man with twenty-two years on a road, and four people he would not have traded for a tier, should come to a fence. It was the same act, and it felt entirely different.
   218	
   219	---
   220	
   221	He had it down to counting by the last quarter mile, and what he counted was the thing on the rise.
   222	
   223	That was the method. He had used it on fighters for two years, from the east tier of a yard, and he had never once tried it on an animal; but he could find no reason why it should not hold. One wrote down what could be counted, and gave a name to nothing else.
   224	
   225	So, first: it kept its station, and he checked that three times. The ruts bent a little to the right, toward the swell, and the grey shape gave ground, drifting up the slope until there were forty-five yards between them again. The ruts bent left, and it came down. Whatever it wanted, it wanted it from exactly that far away, like a watcher who has been told his distance.
   226	
   227	Second: it made no sound. He had known that from the grass. Now he saw the other half of it, which was that the frogs went dumb a little way in front of it and took up again a little way behind, so that it walked the whole time inside the hole that Lira had heard. He did not know how frogs had learned to do that, and he thought that it would take a long time.
   228	
   229	The third thing he nearly missed. Its head was not turned toward the people. All the way up the valley it had been turned a little behind him, toward the cart, and when he looked for the reason he heard it: the buckle. It was the one thing in the whole party that kept time. He lifted his left hand and closed the fist, twice, where Brom would see.
   230	
   231	There was a pause of four strides. Then the clicking stopped, for a large hand had been reached over the tailboard and shut on a strap.
   232	
   233	The grey head swung. It went slowly to one side and then to the other, low, casting, as a dog casts that has lost a line. Brom let go. The buckle clicked, and the head came round and settled on it again.
   234	
   235	*It tracked the buckle.* That was worth having, and it was all that he had. At forty-five yards the grey head had moved when the buckle moved, and had lost it when the buckle stopped; a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth had drawn no answer that Cael could see. He could not say what sense had found the buckle, and the finding told him nothing of what the thing was waiting for.
   236	
   237	The fourth thing was the gravel.
   238	
   239	A gully came down out of the right-hand hill, and at its foot the floods of other years had spread a fan of pale stones clean across the valley, a hundred feet wide, with a skin of water shining on it. The ruts ran straight over. The mule's feet rattled in the wet, and the cart came after with a noise like a sack of nails. Out on the right the grey shape came to the edge of the fan and stopped as if it had met a wall.
   240	
   241	It would not put a foot on it. It turned, and for the first time it ran, a long flat lope up the slope and round the head of the gully, where the stones began; he lost it against the hill, and found it, sixty yards off, and seventy. Then it came down the far side at the same lope, and dropped to a walk, and it was forty-five yards from the road again before the tailboard was clear of the wet.
   242	
   243	He did not know why. He set that down, in his head, with a ruled line under it, for Karis.
   244	
   245	The fifth thing was not a thing it did, and it took him the longest to see.
   246	
   247	It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
   248	
   249	There was a light in front of them.
   250	
   251	The fence stood up out of the grass all at once, as the carter had said it would: split posts, as high as a man's head, from one shoulder of the valley to the other, with a gate in the middle and a lantern coming toward the gate from inside. He heard a bar drawn.
   252	
   253	He looked to the right. The grey shape had stopped.
   254	
   255	It had not sat, or turned. It stood in the thin grass with its head level, beside a white stone, and it came no farther. Cael fixed the stone. Then he lengthened his stride to the yard, as he had learned to do on sixty feet of oak, and counted from abreast of it to the gate, as Karis would have counted, with his lips shut.
   256	
   257	A hundred and ten.
   258	
   259	Nobody had struck a blow, or so much as drawn a knife. Behind him he heard Brom let out a breath that he seemed to have been keeping since the mouth of the valley. The gate swung inward on a leather hinge, and the lantern came up, and behind it he saw a woman's hand.

