editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:157:They looked at him when he came in. Neither of them asked anything. He understood, standing in the doorway with the copy under his arm, that they had been sitting up for hours, and that it was not for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:159:Karis came up a little after. He heard her step on the stair and moved out of the doorway, and she stopped on the threshold with ink to the second knuckle of both hands and her notebook against her chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:103:Karis stood in the doorway and did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:105:Karis was standing in her own doorway at the top, with the old compilations in their three bindings held against her chest, looking at the door as if it had said something to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:She had borrowed Brom's docket out of his left inside pocket to copy its date, and Brom had stood in the annex doorway until she gave it back, which she did inside a quarter of an hour. She had the two dates on a slip, one above the other. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The seat's ruling, before the year's close, and there was only one Fifth-day in the year's last week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:233:He was at the east end of the hall, by the tall windows, alone, standing with his hands clasped behind his back and his head turned a little to one side, the way a man stands in a doorway at night when he thinks he has heard a step on the stair and is waiting to hear it again. The east windows looked down the bluff road as far as the bend above the trees, and anybody who showed at that bend still had two switchbacks to climb before the lodge-man heard a hoof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:153:After a while Ephram came up the other stair with his kit. He looked at Brom from the doorway. Then he went to the frame beside Brom's, the one he had used every evening of the week before the first sitting, and set his feet, and began his entry line, the cut along it, the recovery, at the same pace as Brom's, a hair apart, keeping time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:235:Through the far doorway Cael could see the records table. The officer with the careful face had stayed at it. He was squaring the minute-book to the table's edge with both hands, and then, when it was square, squaring it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:104:He went back for it, a step at a time, as he would have made a witness go back. A tread on the stair that had not been his clerk's tread: single steps, quick, the whole flight, no pause at the top. The clerk in the doorway with the packet held out in both hands. His own face, arranged. Then the two sentences. Then, it seemed, the folder, and the window, and a long while of the porter and the pigeons that had gone into him without leaving any trace at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:126:The clerk had said it from the doorway at the sixth bell, with the day's wallet against his chest, looking not at the desk but past it at the window, where the evening had gone close and grey. It was the only thing he had ever said in that room in nine years that had nothing to do with paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:71:Only the four of them: Lira cross-legged on the window seat, Brom in the doorway because the reading room had three chairs and he would not take one, Cael at the window. One lamp. The girl had been sent to bed and had gone, which she did not always do. On the long table Karis had laid out a sheet on which she had drawn the selling floor of the wool exchange from Bracken's description, with the seat on its platform at the far end and the tables below and the benches behind, and every chair marked in small neat letters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:85:"The gallery," said Brom, from the doorway. "Who comes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:97:He was young, younger than any registry officer Cael had seen in a doorway, and he had a heavy cold. He held a folded handkerchief in the hand that was not holding the board, and between names he turned his head politely away and used it, and turned back, and found his place on the board with one finger. He did not apologize for the cold or for the door or for the people on the steps. He read the list.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:239:Karis came up the stair while he was sanding it, reading her draft aloud to herself as she climbed, the way she did when a sentence would not sit. He heard her in the doorway. *The respondent submits that the integrity sections, read with their own proviso,* and then nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:99:The doorway was in the middle of the afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:107:*There?* said the new thing, about the doorway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:Cael was off the bed without any memory of getting off it; his ribs would tell him about it a long while afterward. He was standing in the middle of the room looking at a doorway with nothing in it, and Lira's face on the far side of the nothing, white, with her shoulder still pressed against the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Lira had stopped pushing against it. She stood in Brom's arm and put her open hand on the nothing in the doorway, at the height of her shoulder, and held it there, and looked at Cael through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:137:He did not try again, because he did not know what else to try. He stood in the middle of his room with his hands open and looked at her, and she looked at him, and the doorway held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:"Twelve seconds," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:"So does your Wind," said Lira, "and nobody blames you when it knocks a tray off a table on the oak." She settled her foot on the stool. "Write that it was in you and it wasn't you. Both. They're both true, and I was the one in the doorway, so I get to say which goes first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:He shut the door, carefully, all the way, until the latch clicked. Then he sat down on the end of the bed, because Lira had the chair, and they were still sitting so when Karis came at the fifth bell with her squared paper and stopped in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and looked at the three of them, and asked what had happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:173:Brom told her, and she listened. Then she came in, and sat on the floor with her back to the wall, and drew a line on her sheet at the bell, and wrote beside it, in her wide crooked left-handed letters, *Doorway. Uncommanded. Twelve seconds. Brom counting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:179:He stood in the doorway with his hands in his coat pockets and did not come in. He looked at Lira's foot on the stool and at Brom's sling and at the strapping round Cael's ribs where his shirt was open, and at the rota nailed to the inside of the door. He read the rota. Cael watched him read it, all of it, down to *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.*, and then look at the doorway he was standing in, as if it had told him something he did not have words for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:209:"The leaf. I sat at the sill with all of you on the Second-day and priced it. I wrote down everything I could think of, the first minute and a week without the band and the lane and the read and somebody in the room. I left blanks where I didn't know, the way Seln said." He looked at the door. "There wasn't a line for that. There wasn't a line that said it might do something on its own, in a doorway, to Lira, with me lying here saying *no*. I didn't think of it. I measured it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:227:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:229:"And the doorway?" said Cael at last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:"The doorway's the fire's," said Brom. "We'll write it down. Next time there's a leaf, it'll have a line for doorways." He shut his eyes again. "Go to sleep. I'm counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:280:The marks went down the sheet in their rows: forty in the first hour she had counted, then thirty-eight, thirty-six, thirty-one after the storm moved, thirty, twenty-eight. A line at the doorway with a box round it. Twenty-two through the night, by Brom's count and Lira's, which they had kept on the backs of their hands. Then the dawn, and the naming, and the counts dropped off the bottom of the page all at once: nine, four, two, one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:302:He did not come in, but stood in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and looked at the rota on the door and the squared paper on Karis's knee, and held out the letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:316:*Not on the leaf: the doorway. Twelve seconds, uncommanded, with Lira in it. Brom says that's the fire's and not the rule's. I'm writing it down so the next leaf has a line for doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:201:Each one was a weight with no body, pressed into the chalk at a single point, as if somebody very heavy were standing there on one toe. The coach had rolled over the three at the west mouth a minute ago without a jolt; they lay flush with the road like stones under the dust, waiting for whatever would be strung between them. They did not move. They did not drain anything, the way Ternhall's anchors had drunk the heat out of Karis's contacts into a floor. They were not strung across the air like the Lattice Path's lines, to be found by walking into them. They were points, and they held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:3:Rooke struck the three bouts before breakfast, standing at the program's board in the east hall's doorway with a stick of chalk, and Cael watched him do it from the foot of the residence stair because his ribs had made the stair a thing to be taken slowly and he had stopped halfway to rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:41:Bracken brought it up himself. He did not have to; the porter's shoulder was mending and Seln was doing the sort, and Seln would have put it in Cael's hole with the rest. But Bracken came up the residence stair with it under his arm at the noon bell, and knocked, and held it out in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and did not come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He stopped in the doorway and stood there with one hand on the frame, a tall loose young man in the cohort's coat with the green-taped captain's file under his arm, because he went nowhere without it. He looked at the four of them. Then he looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:99:He came up the library stair without a sound, as he came up every stair, and stopped in the doorway, and did not come in at once. He stood there with his hands at his sides and looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:At the reading room's door he had felt it ask. He had felt it on every doorway since the road, and this one was a good door, oak, heavy, set true in its frame, the kind of door the new thing wanted most. *There?* He had named it under his breath. *Door. Karis's. It opens.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:227:*Wind-adjacent — Lira, the burst. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere, the push. Iron-adjacent — Brom, the read, which found the fourteen. Compression-adjacent — Reydan, the give. Ember-adjacent — Karis, the spark, two contacts and no more. Shadow-adjacent — the stair, sealed, rent from waking. Storm-adjacent — Daeva, the lane; moved twice this week, a quarter hour and then forty minutes, and settled. Anchor-adjacent — the hand with no face; idle by naming; wants doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:He did it with the stub of pencil from behind his ear, standing in his own doorway, while the last of the four horses was still going down the bluff road below the gate, because his book was on the counter behind him and he did not want to lose the minute in the turning round. Cael saw him do it from the wall stair, coming down. Then the old man went in, and took the book off the counter, and copied the figure off his hand into it, and licked his thumb and rubbed the hand clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:41:The clerk wrote that too. He did not look at Cael in the doorway, and neither did Bracken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:43:Gault was in the lodge doorway beside Cael. Nobody had seen him come across the court; he had his case clamped under one arm and a small black notebook in his other hand, and he had evidently been standing there for the last two entries.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:47:Bracken gave it to the clerk, and the clerk wrote it. Gault opened his own notebook on his forearm and wrote in it too, standing in the doorway, a short line, and closed it on the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:109:"Before anybody says anything about her," she said, "I want the words straight, because they're going to blur if we're not careful, and I'll be the one keeping the notes." She held up one finger. "Ternhall's anchors are mine. Three footholds I plant in a floor, in their order, so that the heat of a contact runs down into the boards and not back up my arm. Ternhall calls the whole frame a lattice." A second finger. "Which has nothing to do with the Lattice Path. That's lines you can't see, strung across a space, that you find by walking into them." She lowered the hand. "And then there's whatever stood in our court this morning. It wasn't either of those. It didn't plant anything and it didn't string anything. I haven't got a word for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:"Anchor Path," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:129:Cael wrote *Anchor Path* in the notebook under the morning's page, and under it Karis's three words in her order, and ruled a line beneath them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:201:Seln turned his cups. He always had. A quarter-turn when he sat down, a quarter-turn back when he had said what he meant to say, a quarter-turn on the counter when he had finished a cup of the lodge-man's tea. Cael had watched him do it for two years without ever once remarking on it. Tonight the cup had stood at his elbow for a whole evening, through Anchor Path and the rubbing and Bracken's letter, and he had not turned it once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:117:He knew what she was doing as soon as he saw it, because he had heard her explain the difference on the night of the gate, counting the words off on her fingers. Ternhall's anchors were her own, three footholds that drank the heat of a contact down into a floor. The Lattice Path was lines strung across the air. These were neither. These were points, and they held. But something ran between them. When she had put her hand out to the horse's leg she had found it, the way you find a rope across a dark yard: a line, taut, between the point fixed against the horse's foreleg and one of the fourteen in the bank. And she had put her heat into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:He stood a moment in the shop doorway with the twine in his hand, out of the rain, and watched the street. A dray going over the bridge with its load sheeted. Two boys with a dog. A woman under a shawl buying eels from a barrow. The bridge-ward in his oilskin at the near end of the parapet, looking down at the water against the old piers and then at the iron rings let into the stone of the middle one, the way a man looks at a clock he expects to be late. There were five rings on that pier, one above the other. The river was at the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:183:She did not hurry across the court. She walked to the lodge and stopped beside the lodge-man in his doorway, and before she said a word to anybody, she lifted her eyes to the board on the lodge wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:217:Four riders, a chancellor, the house's counsel with her hair coming down, the lodge-man in his doorway, and up on the gatehouse a boy behind a bell-frame, all of them stood and waited while the registrar read a seal with his eyes shut. It took him a minute and forty seconds. Cael counted it. It was a long time to stand still in a court; Bracken had earned every second of it at his counter, and the riders seemed to know that, because none of them shifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:He did not sit down to say it, and he did not raise his voice. He stood at the middle of the table's far side with the whole of the case laid out in front of him in the lamplight, the forty and the bundle and the two stacks and the leaf, and he said it to the room the way he had once read a form to a man in a doorway at the third hour, one plain sentence after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:157:Seln stood on the landing with his coat buttoned to the throat and something square under it, and he did not come in. He stood in the doorway and looked at the room in the order Cael knew. The door, the lock, the card; the two windows, the lit one with its lamp, the narrow one at the end, dark, with the library's roof below it; the landing behind him, the annex door, the stair; the bare table and the shaded lamp; then the four of them, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:At Norhold the wrong thing had answered. Here nothing answered at all. He knelt in the dust and called his own legs, out of habit, to get him up, and the calling went out of him and found nobody at home. The Wind did not lift its head. The read lay where he had left it. Everything he carried had turned, all together and very politely, to look at the new thing standing in the doorway, and none of them was listening to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:[unnamed] — Anchor-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:38:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:284:The house's healer was a spare grey woman who had strapped every fighter on the hill for twenty years and had long ago stopped asking how anybody had come by anything. She looked at the five of them in her doorway, in their chalk, and sent Seln away at once, because there was nothing wrong with him but wet knees, and set the other four out along her benches in the order she meant to deal with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He had the line laid already. He did not cut at her; he simply turned his wrist, and the lath came up and lay along the path she was travelling, like a bar dropped across a doorway, and she arrived on it. It took her on the forearm, the left, the one she carried close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:He walked. It was the old walk, at every angle, with the lath low, but he was not walking to learn weight now. He was laying lines. He turned his wrist and there was one across the middle of the oak, level, a bar across a doorway, and he turned it again and there was a second, slantwise, from the rim wall to the first. He could not hold more than two at once; Cael saw that, and saw that he did not need to. He was building a corner out of two lines and four feet of sunk stone wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:43:That was what she had been braced for, all the way up the stair. It was a room with a board in it and a row of backs, and it had a door, and she had expected the old cold to come up through her boots, the cold that had lived in her knees since she was a girl. She had stood in a doorway there with a paper in her hand, filled in correctly, in somebody else's writing, and been put on the outside of that door by people who were being perfectly polite.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:It left each one. He had a stair built for this by now, and the asking came up it and took a name and settled. The other one, the one from the chalk road, said nothing all morning, for there were no doorways on a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:121:He told it the way he had written it, standing against a wall under a lamp in the covered walk with the notebook held to the stone at the third hour. The archive at Halcenvane on a bad night two years ago, near enough, in the first year he was on the hill. The fire-watch gone the long way round. A man with a bag at the top of the upper flight, in the doorway, with his gloved hand already going into a press he had no business opening. And on the landing above him, in the light, the wing's assistant. He was reading the man a form, one plain line after another, about copying and paper and what a magistrate counted in years, until the man's hand came back out of the press with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:129:"I knew there was somebody in the corner on the second night, and I knew who on the third." Seln's voice had not changed at all. "I did not look down the flight. A man in a doorway with a bag looks where the officer looks, so I did not look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:156:"A year of it," said Seln. "Before I was a man in a doorway, I glowed." He did not explain that, and Brom, in the chair by the shelf, looked at the floor as if he had heard it before somewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:111:She could not drill. She went every dawn regardless and sat at the rail with Rooke's red drill-book and watched the program work under the two lamps, because she said a drill watched was half a drill done, and that she would rather be half than nothing. This morning she did not go to the rail by the wall. She stood a moment in the doorway with her hand on the frame. Then she let go and walked across the painted floor between the white crosses and arrows to where Rooke stood at the far rail with his arms folded, in front of the whole program, at her own pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:He came into the reading room with a flat packet under his arm wrapped in oilcloth and sealed with the Greyvane archive's grey wax, and stood in the doorway in his spectacles and weighed it on his palm out of habit before he held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:She had the bench by the rail, with Rooke's red drill-book on her knee for something to hold and her right foot out straight on the boards. Karis had chalk and her grey notebook and her coat buttoned right for once. Brom had gone up the gallery stair and drawn every shutter down the length of the north wall, as Lira had drawn them on the first honest floor of the year. Now he stood in the hall's great doorway, with one of the two tall doors standing open a hand's breadth onto the dark of the passage and his right hand on its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:269:Brom moved his weight in the doorway. Cael felt it before he heard it: a rest lifting, and another place on the boards, half a pace to the left, where a foot was about to come down, lit faintly a breath before the foot came. He opened his eyes in time to see Brom's boot come down there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:277:He had stood in the doorway all this while with his right hand on the edge of the open door, because somebody had to be in the door, and he had noticed something that Cael had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:307:Cael stood in the middle of the painted floor and looked at Brom's hand on the edge of the door, the big square hand that had held twelve feet of road against two men on the Ostrand road and held a girl up in a doorway for twelve seconds. Something in him went quiet that had nothing to do with the new thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:329:"It wants the line," said Cael. "It thinks it's a doorway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:331:"Two points and a line between them," said Karis. "That's all a doorway is, to it, isn't it? Jamb and jamb and the space between." She wrote it. "Name it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:349:The chalk line at twelve feet lay on the boards between its two small fires, and the new thing, which had been faint about it, was suddenly not faint. It leaned. It leaned the way it had leaned in his own doorway with Lira coming through it on her crutch, when he had been halfway through saying *no*. Something in him began to go out of him toward the line, quick and quiet as a breath, to set a point at one end of it and a point at the other and tell the space between them to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:111:Cael stood in the doorway and looked at it in Seln's order before he knew he was doing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:219:He gave her the whole of his attention before he answered. It was the look he had given the room from the doorway, slow and level and entire, the look of a man who has been sent to evaluate something and means to do it fairly even when the something is a girl with a turned ankle asking him a straight question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:271:He thought of the room in Ostrand, though he had told himself on the post road that he would not. Four young people and a man at a window. The man had stood a little to one side of the glass, where the frame hid him from the lane, the way the field service teaches its people to stand, and had copied forty orders in a round level hand that never once let itself be hurried. The Archmarshal had known what he was from the doorway. He had known, reading, roughly what the copying must have cost and how many years it had sat somewhere before it came to that table. He did not know the man's name, and he had been careful all the way home not to wonder it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:291:He had a file under his arm, a different one, and a question about a guild hall's frame that took a minute to ask and a minute to answer. When it was answered he did not go. He stood in the doorway with the file against his chest, nervous and good, and looked past the Archmarshal at the tray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:311:At the sixth bell his clerk stood in the doorway with the day's wallet against his chest, looking not at the desk but past it, at the window, where the evening had come in grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:141:Cael stood in the doorway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:299:They stood at the ring's far edge, with the gelding blowing a little, and Cael waited for the thing in him to ask. It asked at every threshold. It had asked at gates and doorways and the lip of a ford, and at a sill with two sockets, that very morning; and he stood with his boots at the place where a carter said the world's paper ended, and listened for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:189:In the doorway the officer stood and watched them. He was pleased. Anybody could have seen it. He had done a thing he had done ten thousand times, and done it properly, and a child was happy, and he liked his work. He looked at his board, and turned it over, and went in to see who was next.

