     1	# Chapter 7 — Thirteenth
     2	
     3	They stayed six days at Thornwater, and went down on the forty-eighth morning in front of an empty cart.
     4	
     5	The carter was going back for his next load, and the boxes rode with him again, on a bed of folded sacks, since nobody had been asked to pay for them the first time. He took the stretch at noon. Cael walked the mile of it with the sun on the crown of his head, in the left-hand rut, with his knife on his belt and all nine of his hands held down; and he saw grass, and a split cart-shaft, and a white stone with nothing beside it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other and did not stop anywhere.
     6	
     7	They came over the saddle above Lowmarch in the afternoon of the forty-ninth day, and he knew before they were off the hill that the meadow had changed.
     8	
     9	There were twice the tents, for the dry roads had done it, as the carter had said they would: crews had come in from every quarter while the five were away, and the grass along the river was trodden brown, and three fires burned where there had been one. A knot of people stood at the plank's near end, under the long eave, as people stand at a counter when a clerk is giving out results.
    10	
    11	Pike had the book open and the stone on it. He looked up as they came along the eave, and looked again, and put his finger on a leaf without having to turn to it.
    12	
    13	"You've read it," he said. "He gave it you."
    14	
    15	"Yes."
    16	
    17	"Is it wrong?"
    18	
    19	"No."
    20	
    21	Pike nodded, and turned his shoulder to the meadow, and raised his level, carrying voice over the heads of the people at the plank, so that it went as far as the ash. The Thornwater line went out exactly as it had come to them on bark.
    22	
    23	It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
    24	
    25	Pike unlocked the iron box and counted nine coins onto the oak, in threes. Karis took them up, since the purse was hers to keep. She did not count them a second time in front of him, which Cael knew she would do later.
    26	
    27	Pike did not wait for her to finish. Another slip lay ready under the edge of his stone, where somebody had left it for him earlier in the day. He drew it out with two fingers, and read it to himself, and read it again, and he was giving it to the meadow before the last of their silver was off the oak.
    28	
    29	"North road. Four carts of salt and iron walked to the upper holds. Delivered at the last gate. One mule lamed. Fourteen marks."
    30	
    31	"Lame when I took him."
    32	
    33	It came from behind Cael's shoulder, in no louder a voice than Pike's own. He turned, and found a lean, burnt woman with a strip of red cloth tied round her hair, standing a little way out from the eave with her arms folded. He had taken her for one of the crowd.
    34	
    35	Pike looked at her, in his careful double fashion.
    36	
    37	"Which foot?"
    38	
    39	"Near fore. He was pointing it in the yard before a pack went on. His own carter will tell you."
    40	
    41	Pike lifted his head and called a name across the grass, and a man at the second fire stood up with a bowl in his hand. He was asked one question, and he answered it with a nod and sat down again.
    42	
    43	Two men and a girl got up off the bank then, where they had been sitting with their packs between their feet, and came to stand at the woman's back. Cael saw that it was a captain who had spoken, and that her crew had left her to say it alone until it was proved.
    44	
    45	"One mule lame at taking," said Pike, and dipped his pen.
    46	
    47	He wrote it so, and when the ink was on the page he straightened, and turned his shoulder, and said the whole line through once more, from *north road* to *paid*, with the four words changed. Only then did he go to the box.
    48	
    49	"Why twice?" said Lira.
    50	
    51	She had held it in since the fourteenth day, and Cael supposed that five weeks was a fair trial of anybody. Pike did not seem to mind, and went on counting silver for the woman with the red cloth.
    52	
    53	"Once before it's in, so she can say it's wrong. Once after, so it's said."
    54	
    55	"Said to who? It's written. It's in your book."
    56	
    57	Pike looked past her, down the length of the meadow. There were perhaps two hundred people in it that afternoon, cooking and mending and arguing over mules, and Cael watched him consider them.
    58	
    59	"Half this meadow can't read. The book's for me. The saying's for them."
    60	
    61	He pushed the last coin across.
    62	
    63	Cael had been written in a good many books. There was one in a district office with a single word against his name, and one that a mild man had filled for two years, and one on a shelf somewhere with slips in its pocket that no officer would own; and the thing they had all had in common was that they were shut. People had read them about him in rooms he was not let into, and he had stood on a carved number for fourteen sittings and never once been shown the page.
    64	
    65	This book lay open on a plank in the weather, and its keeper stood up and shouted it at anybody who was frying dough.
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	The man after her was a big one, in a new coat, and he was pleased with himself.
    70	
    71	He had brought fleece down from the high fold. Cael remembered the slip, for it had hung on the plank the day they came: eleven bales, at a mark the bale, delivered dry. The big man laid it down with a slap. Somebody at the landing had put a row of strokes on it for a witness, eleven of them, and through the last a short bar had been drawn.
    72	
    73	Pike read it twice, and then he gave it out.
    74	
    75	"Fleece, down from the high fold. Eleven bales carried. Ten delivered dry, and one wet. Ten marks."
