editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:5:The carter was going back for his next load, and the boxes rode with him again, on a bed of folded sacks, since nobody had been asked to pay for them the first time. He took the stretch at noon. Cael walked the mile of it with the sun on the crown of his head, in the left-hand rut, with his knife on his belt and all nine of his hands held down; and he saw grass, and a split cart-shaft, and a white stone with nothing beside it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other and did not stop anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:23:It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:63:Cael had been written in a good many books. There was one in a district office with a single word against his name, and one that a mild man had filled for two years, and one on a shelf somewhere with slips in its pocket that no officer would own; and the thing they had all had in common was that they were shut. People had read them about him in rooms he was not let into, and he had stood on a carved number for fourteen sittings and never once been shown the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:Cael did not understand it for the space of a breath, and then he did, and the hair stood on his arms. It was no threat, for Pike would write it, exactly, as he wrote everything. He would put down that a captain said eleven dry, and beside it that a witness at the landing had marked ten; and then he would stand up and say both to two hundred people, and say them again when the ink was on. It would be in the book for as long as there was a book, and every hirer who ever asked about that crew would be told that its captain had once stood at this plank and argued with a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:209:Cael opened his mouth to say, and found that he had no way of telling it from here. It was west, beyond a line and a hill and a river and the whole of a country, at the far end of everything he knew; and the best keeper of a straight book that he had met out here had never so much as heard the town's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:247:He had told them, on a shelf of turf, that he knew a certain phrase, having copied it onto other people's files; and none of them had asked him then what it kept in its drawer. He put one finger down on the hide's western edge, on bare skin, an inch beyond the place where the carter's ruts ran out, and he kept it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:315:Two carts gone, a month back. He knew the stretch, and what that mile asked of a person's hands, and he knew that the ford on that road was a worse place than the grass. It wanted clearing, and it wanted clearing by somebody who would not lose a man doing it; and there were, by his reckoning, two crews on the sheet who could, and the other was up the north road until the new moon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:317:So it was his, and it must wait a week, since frost would not. He had weighed the one against the other and chosen, and he did not go back over a thing he had weighed; but a held slip was a kind of debt. If that ford took anybody while he was on a ledge with a string of salt, it would be on his name, whether Pike wrote it there or not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:323:He had heard their four lines said at the plank, or heard of them: a boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon. And today the stretch, walked at dusk, by the carter's own telling, with a hound pacing them for half the mile; and not one of the five had so much as lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:349:He was the youngest of them. He had a book in front of him, shut on a pencil, and he sat quite still in the long light from the door with his hands on the cover; and he was not staring, as boys stared who wanted a place in a crew. He was reading. Teague could see the order of it. The boots. The wrist, and the leather on the wrist. The three in the doorway behind, one at a time, left to right. He was going over a stranger as Teague himself went over ground, and he was not troubling to hide it, any more than Teague was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:363:He was a man of forty or so, of the middle height, heavy in the forearm, with hair cropped close and gone grey over the ears. He had stopped on the sill before he came in. That was the first item, and Cael put the most weight on it. Three people had stopped behind him in the same instant, at the same distances, without a word; and for the length of a breath the man's eyes had gone nowhere at all, while he listened to them be there. He had counted his own before he counted the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:Brom's left arm took it. The knuckles went white and the shoulder stood out through his shirt, and the ladder bowed like a drawn bow and came back; and when he had found the rung with his foot he went on up, more slowly, and set the sack down in the loft before he looked at his own hands. They were brown, both of them. Iron Skin had not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:133:He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does, and he may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:143:The first was the road they had come by, and the second went north from a wicket in the fence, up to grazing, and ended at a stone hut with nobody in it. The third left by a gate on the eastern side and went on up the valley, over a low place in the hills. He had walked two hours along that one, yesterday, with the sun on his head. It was a road like the others, with ruts that had been used, though not lately; and nobody had told him that it was different from the rest, and nobody had told him that it was the same. He turned back at the two hours because he had promised himself that he would be within a fence, any fence, well before the light began to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:227:The carter had said it as he might have said that the hay was in. *That's the season open.* He had meant the roads, and the weather on them, and a board in a meadow with slips enough on it for every crew that could walk; and he could not have known what the word had been to the person he said it to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:229:Cael had lived by seasons since he was fourteen, and he had not owned one of them. The first had been the registry's, a round of assessments in a district office, and he had been carried through it like a parcel that nobody could find the right shelf for. After that it had been a circuit's, and a tournament's, with a bracket drawn by other people. Then it was a house's, an academic year, with a bell in it; and the last had been a seat's, under a hood, fourteen sittings long. Each of them had opened on a day that somebody else appointed, and each had closed with a paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:The second time she pulled up on the line itself, stumbling, with her weight all wrong and her face dark with temper, and said that it was like running hard at a door and then leaving it shut. The third time she ran as she must have run on a painted floor with a coach watching. She came down the bar like something thrown; and three strides short of the line the air cracked, and the gravel went up in a fan, and she was standing twelve feet beyond his mark with her hands out in front of her and the heron lifting off the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:63:Karis sat on the bank above them with a river stone in her lap and one finger resting on it. She had explained it to him in three sentences, as she explained anything. To set a point was not to light it; she chose the place and held it ready, like a thumb on a latch; and the task was to hold it so. She counted under her breath. At ninety a flame no bigger than a seed stood up on the wet stone, and she took her finger away and looked at it with dislike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:73:Seln was not on the bar. He was never anywhere at first light now, and came back when the meadow was stirring, with dust to his ankles and nothing to say about it; and Cael, who had spent two years being watched by that man, had decided to let him have his mornings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:99:*Anchor. Unnamed; the hand with no face. Fixed points, and lines between them, and a cage; and one unbinding, done once, on purpose. It sees what is fixed: sure at ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen. I don't know its reach, or its rate, or its price. Ever since the Ostrand road it has asked about every sill and gate and doorway I've gone through, and a town with no wall has a great many doors.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:175:Karis had not moved. Her hand was still out, over the wood, and her mouth had come a little open; and Cael, who had sat beside her through fifty-one minutes in front of a hooded seat, watched her want something more than she had wanted to win that day. He saw her mouth close, and her hand come back into her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:219:Cael felt it coming this time. The sun went behind the western ridge, all at once, as it did; the river turned from brass to lead; and he had dug holes for two days and his shoulders ached to the bone. It lifted behind the rest of what he carried, soft, and offered him the old thing. He had the refusal ready before it finished asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:That was the part he could not get used to, for a return was a thing with a desk at the far end of it. He had made seven hundred of them, more or less, and at the end of each there had been somebody who asked for it, in a level voice, across a table; and he had given what could be checked and kept back all the rest, so evenly and for so long that it had been mistaken for a manner of writing. He had five roads in his head now, entire, down to the paces, and they were better than anything he had ever sent in, for he had left nothing out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:279:The sun was on the river when he came out above the meadow. Four people were on the gravel bar. One of them ran, and stopped dead on a line, on her two feet, and flung up both arms; and a large one carrying a tree let it roll from his shoulder and stepped out from under it as neatly as a dancer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:There was an alder on the bank that would have covered him. He stood clear of it, in the open, with his hands where they could be seen, until the large one looked up and found him and raised an arm; and then he went down to them with five roads in his head, and found that he was still waiting, and did not altogether mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:327:Cael saw the grey start at both wrists, and he saw it stop. Then Brom did the only thing that a very large man can do who has decided to be nothing more than that: he sat down. He sat down hard in the dirt of the ramp with the rope round his body and his heels dug in, and was dragged a yard on the seat of his breeches, and another foot, cutting two furrows; and there, like an anchor that has found bottom, he held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:347:The woman with the hides scratched her mark on the slip against her own tailboard. She had not spoken a word in three hours. She looked now at Brom, who was red earth from his heels to his belt, and at Lira's hands; and last she looked at Cael, for a good while, with her head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:355:She thought about that, and she neither took her own words back nor argued with his; and then she clicked her tongue twice at her mules.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:11:He was a short, thick man, burnt as brown as a nut, and his cart was loaded to the rails with sacks. He chocked his wheels, saw to the mule, and sat down in the ring with a yard of empty ground on either side of him. Cael had seen him once already that day, at noon, handing a slip across the plank; and he had watched the meadow read that slip all afternoon, crew after crew, and go back to its dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:49:Cael did what he always did, and began with the claim, which was a mile of road that carters crossed only at one hour of the day. Under it stood two carts that had not come home, and a price that the whole meadow thought too small, and a word, *quiet*, that everybody out here used for a thing done and also for a thing that happened to a cart. He had no notion what walked on that stretch; but a man who had kept the road for twenty-two years had just said what he wanted for it, and it was the one thing they had spent a month learning to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:He meant a Path. He meant Lira's burst, and Brom's Iron Skin, and any point that Karis might set; and Cael knew, with a coldness in his stomach, that he meant nine fragments as well, which were nobody's Path at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:181:The ground rose a little there, on the right, in a low stony swell where the grass grew thin; and something came up out of the tall stuff onto that rise and went along the top of it, where the last of the light lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:197:Ten strides later a stone turned under her heel. It was nothing, a loose cobble in the rut, such as any of them had trodden on forty times that day; but her whole weight went sideways, and in the instant before she had her balance he felt the air beside him tighten, as it tightens in a yard before somebody goes. Her hand shot out and shut on his sleeve. She did not go. She hung there for one stride with her fingers twisted in his coat and her teeth showing, and then she had her foot under her, and let go of him, and smoothed the cloth down where she had gripped it as though that had been all she meant to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:223:That was the method. He had used it on fighters for two years, from the east tier of a yard, and he had never once tried it on an animal; but he could find no reason why it should not hold. One wrote down what could be counted, and gave a name to nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:247:It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:19:It was Seln who had asked, mildly, from his place a little behind the rest; and when they turned he was looking at the eastern saddle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:Brom made a sound that he turned into a cough, and Lira looked round at the four of them with her mouth gone very tight at one corner; and then, since nothing else could be done with it, she laughed. It was not a long laugh, and when it was over she wiped her nose on her wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:129:The smith shrugged, and then sold Lira a long knife, which was the last thing the old purse ever bought. She chose it by its balance, across two fingers, as she would have chosen a lath; and when he told her that it was a skinning blade she said that it was whatever she said it was, and wore it home on her hip, outside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:171:Lira pressed hers first, hard, and Karis followed her, and Brom, whose print took up a third of the strip, and Cael; and then the wax was cooling, and one of them had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:279:The woman with the dough had gone back to her pan. She had heard it, though, and so had the man with the harness across his knees, and the three children; and by dark, Cael supposed, the whole meadow would know that five strangers had stood on a boat all day and lost nothing. It was not much to be known for; but it was the first thing that anybody east of a line knew about them at all, and it happened to be true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:17:He looked for the wall first, since every town he had ever entered had kept one, even Denvash, which was nothing; and a wall meant a gate, and a gate meant a man in it with a book. He looked for the station next, the low stone house with the windowless building behind, and for a board of hours, and for any roof with a seal burned into its sign. His eye went over the place three times in search of them, like a tongue going back to where a tooth has been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:45:The carts lay along the river side with their shafts down and a stone behind each wheel. The tents were farther in, in no order he could find, of hide and tarred canvas and old sail, and they were not the same tents from one hour to the next. While Brom was getting the gelding out of the shafts a crew of four struck one within thirty yards of him, rolled it, and walked out by the north road with it on a pole between two of them. Before the flattened grass had lifted, a woman with a handcart had pitched on the same place. He did not see anybody ask anybody's leave. He had counted seventeen tents from the ferry, out of habit, and there were twenty when he counted again; and by evening only nine of the first seventeen stood where they had stood at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:He went after her. Behind him Karis had lifted a corner of the canvas and was counting to three, and Brom was doing some arithmetic of his own, with his lips; and when Cael looked for the fifth of them, he could find no dark coat anywhere in the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:Every name on the board in Ardenmere had carried letters after it, and a number: *Cu 3*, and the like. They had been ratings, borrowed from the registry's tiers and kept in somebody's private ledger, and a stranger could not have stood in that yard for a minute without being told where he stood among them. He had learned to read a card by them before he learned anything else; and here there was only a job, and whoever wanted it done, and what they would pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:203:Pike wrote it and did not ask for the rest. There was a rest, long and correct, with a hyphen in it, and it stood on a withdrawal leaf in a registrar's hall and on a file that had not been tied; but nobody in this meadow had any use for it, and Cael had none either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:69:There was as much of it under the shelf as Brom had said, and it was no kinder than what lay above. The boxes went down a leg at a time, and the cart followed on its locked wheels with a large man leaning back on a rope; and Cael went ahead with the wedge, his back to the slope, and said where the edge was. He said it forty times that morning, and Blade agreed with every one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:73:It had looked like a thread from the ridge. It was a river, forty feet of fast brown water over stones, the colour of weak tea, and so loud after the silence of the hill that Lira had to say everything twice. Trees stood along it, which he had seen from above as a darkness; and they were a darkness still, tall and close, with bark as red as a fox and a smell he could put no name to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:So they laid their own fire in it. Seln's flint was at the bottom of his coat. Karis put out one finger and lit the kindling with a point no bigger than a pin's head, as she had lit a page for Cael under a cart two nights before. The red wood caught fast and burned with a high clear flame; and its smoke stood up out of the valley, straight, in the still air, to twice the height of the trees, where anything with eyes on any of seven ridges might have looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:131:It was a wide place, a hundred feet across and more, where the two streams ran together over shingle, and from the bank it looked no deeper than a boot. A cairn stood on the near side, a neat one, waist high, a good forty yards upstream of where the ruts went in; and there was another on the far bank, as far downstream.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:155:Cael understood the second thing a moment after the first. An animal drinking stirs up the bed, and a ford is a thing people read by its colour; so one watered below the line, and left the crossing clean for whoever came next. Nobody had told her that he needed it explained, and she had not explained it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:207:Other ruts came into theirs out of side valleys, one after another, like streams joining; and by the fifth day's noon the track was a cart wide and beaten hard, with the grass gone from its middle. The valley had opened. The red trees stood back on the slopes, and there was pale grass in the bottom, shoulder high, that went over in the wind like a cat under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:245:He did not notice for a stride, and then the gravel was not answering him. He put his left foot down on loose stones and they took it in silence, like wool, and his right the same; and he looked down at his own feet walking a hard road with no more noise than a cat makes on a stair, and for one stride more he let it be, out of pure astonishment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:287:They did not talk. He noticed that first, and it was a good hour before he believed it. An old man led, with two mules, and behind him came a boy of fifteen or so with one, and then the woman; and when the old man wanted the boy he did not call back to him, but lifted a hand, and the boy looked. Once the woman clicked her tongue, twice, and the old man slowed. From the middle of the morning until the sun stood in the west Cael heard no word spoken above the creak of the wheels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:331:The claim was that out here nobody on a road declared a Path, at any tier, for any purpose; or lit a fire he could do without, or spoke where a hand would serve; and that everybody showed steel.

