editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:"That's what I wanted you to see. I couldn't have told you." He walked down to her. "Out here a burst is a shout. I don't know who it is that listens, and I don't think Brom's deer were telling us, but every carter on that road believes somebody does. And yours has just shouted without asking you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:49:"That's a shout," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:65:"Ninety," she said. "I've never kept one waiting half so long. I've never had cause. I set them without thinking, on a doorpost, or the corner of a page. I should think I've left a dozen standing behind me in every room I ever worked in." She wrote the figure down. "And I don't know whether a point that's set and not lit is a thing this country hears. Nobody has told us what the rule is a rule against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:89:*Iron, from Brom: the read. A pace and a half round me, the mass of a thing and which way it is going, never what it means to do. The cheapest and quietest thing I own, and the hardest to leave shut, because it feels like looking. I don't know what looking sounds like out here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:95:*Shadow. Source protected, and sealed against anyone who might carry the seeing west. It costs from the moment I wake and it drifts at dusk; on the fifth day it got as far as my boots. Two reasons to keep it shut now, where there was one: the seal, and whatever it is a road hears.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:123:"No. It gets quicker. On the hill, at the end, I didn't hear myself doing it; it was only a kind of leaning back, all day." He shut the book on the pencil. "I thought that was the worst of what they'd done to me, that I'd got so I could do it in my sleep. I'd have given a good deal not to be good at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:157:Lira dug. She detested it, and said so at every hole, and was better at it than Cael by a long way, since she had a fighter's sense of where to put her weight. By evening they had eighteen posts in and the old fence drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:169:The old man had come along the line without a sound. He stood six feet behind her with his thumbs in his belt, and he was not angry, any more than a man is angry who tells a child that a pan is hot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:207:He shut his gate, and they heard the bar go across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:227:"I heard it." Seln was a little ahead, on the verge, and his voice was hardly more than breath. "I was waiting to learn whether you would tell me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:335:"Three notches," said Karis. "And there's a sound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:341:She ran down the ramp instead, and went through the water, which was over her ankles now and the colour of the banks, and freed the loop with both hands, and ran back up the way she had come. It took her a count of twenty where a burst would have taken one. From any ridge in that country, nobody would have heard a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:23:It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:65:This book lay open on a plank in the weather, and its keeper stood up and shouted it at anybody who was frying dough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:181:It was a low room, longer than it was wide, with beams gone black and a hearth at the far end that a man could have stood in. Benches ran down both sides, with thirty or forty people on them, by crews, each crew at its own boards, and the noise dropped a little as the five came through the door and then rose again. He was looked at once, and not twice, by people who had heard a line said about him two hours ago and wanted nothing more just yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:199:"Did you ever hear of a ledger-keeper called Vell?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:209:Cael opened his mouth to say, and found that he had no way of telling it from here. It was west, beyond a line and a hill and a river and the whole of a country, at the far end of everything he knew; and the best keeper of a straight book that he had met out here had never so much as heard the town's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:213:"Then I'd not have." Pike drank. "If she keeps it straight, it doesn't want me to have heard of her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:277:*Asked Pike whether he had heard of Vell. No. Seln put a finger on the west edge of the map and said what's there. Once. The map is turned east now.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:303:He did it without turning his head. He had done it so for longer than the boy had been able to lift a pack, at every door and gate and ford, and he no longer knew that he was doing it until it was done. The spear was on his left, half a pace back, grounded. The bow was on his right and a full pace back, standing on the good leg, as the bow always stood when there had been a day's walking. The boy was between and behind, where he had been told to be, and by the sound of him he was already looking for the table with the food on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:323:He had heard their four lines said at the plank, or heard of them: a boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon. And today the stretch, walked at dusk, by the carter's own telling, with a hound pacing them for half the mile; and not one of the five had so much as lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:41:"It's what there is." He looked past her, at the rest of them, and his eyes stopped longest on Brom. "You're the ones had her wagon out of the wash. Before the water. And never lit, she says, nor shouted, nor one of you did a thing but pull."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:The carter had heard her. Out here, it seemed, everybody heard everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:97:They camped cold, in a hollow, a good way back from a stream. Lira and Seln took the first watch and Brom and Karis the second. It was Cael's night to be the fifth, which he spent lying on his back, hearing every sound the scrub made and failing to sleep through any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:151:For the first quarter of a mile there was nothing but the noise, and Cael had not known until then how much noise a quiet valley makes. There were frogs. The bottom was wet, somewhere under the grass, and they were in it by the hundred, a creaking that rose and fell like something breathing. There were crickets, nearer, in a dry steady saw, and some small bird that ticked. Over all of it the wind went through a mile of standing grass with a sound like a hand drawn slowly down a coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:She did not stop walking. She only raised her right hand to the height of her shoulder, with the fingers shut, and held it there. She had spent her life listening to moving air and knew its sounds better than a carter knows his mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:165:It took him a dozen strides to hear it, because it was a thing that was not there. Out on the right, forty or fifty yards into the grass, the frogs had stopped. They had not stopped everywhere. They had stopped in one place, a patch no wider than a room, and all round the edge of that place they creaked as before. It was a hole in the noise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:"Don't declare anything," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might, for all she knew, be a declaration too. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:227:Second: it made no sound. He had known that from the grass. Now he saw the other half of it, which was that the frogs went dumb a little way in front of it and took up again a little way behind, so that it walked the whole time inside the hole that Lira had heard. He did not know how frogs had learned to do that, and he thought that it would take a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:229:The third thing he nearly missed. Its head was not turned toward the people. All the way up the valley it had been turned a little behind him, toward the cart, and when he looked for the reason he heard it: the buckle. It was the one thing in the whole party that kept time. He lifted his left hand and closed the fist, twice, where Brom would see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:*It tracked the buckle.* That was worth having, and it was all that he had. At forty-five yards the grey head had moved when the buckle moved, and had lost it when the buckle stopped; a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth had drawn no answer that Cael could see. He could not say what sense had found the buckle, and the finding told him nothing of what the thing was waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:247:It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:251:The fence stood up out of the grass all at once, as the carter had said it would: split posts, as high as a man's head, from one shoulder of the valley to the other, with a gate in the middle and a lantern coming toward the gate from inside. He heard a bar drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:259:Nobody had struck a blow, or so much as drawn a knife. Behind him he heard Brom let out a breath that he seemed to have been keeping since the mouth of the valley. The gate swung inward on a leather hinge, and the lantern came up, and behind it he saw a woman's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:19:"You can't hear a man count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:"I can hear you stop breathing at the end of each one." Brom spoke low, as he had been taught in an orchard. "That's three. It's zero. I've checked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:47:"I counted three times, and there's nobody." He heard how it sounded. "I don't know what to do with the end of the count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:221:The gelding's shoes rang on the stones. It had not mattered on the upland, where there was turf, and Cael had not heard it in the ruts. But the road was hard now, and wherever rock came through it the iron struck, and the valley gave the sound back. He caught Seln listening to it, with his head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:223:"He can be heard from that ridge," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"So has a mule, and a mule goes unshod on this ground. I have been looking at the prints for two days, as the large one has, and I do not think that anybody on this road has ever wished to be heard from a ridge." He considered the gelding without ill will. "I do not say he is wrong. I say he is an announcement."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:235:They went on late, since the light held. Cael walked last, behind the tailboard, where the dust was; he had taken that place because his arm was sound again and he thought it his turn, and because from the back he could see all four of them and the road behind as well. He had not stopped looking at the road behind; he had only stopped expecting anything of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:243:His boots stopped making any sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:Then he shut it, as he would have shut a lid, with his whole hand. The stones spoke again under his heel, loudly and at once, and he had never been so glad of a small ugly sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:255:"You have it now. For four strides you had not, and by the second it was both feet." Seln walked on a little. When he spoke again it was no louder, and the others, at the horse's head, could not have heard. "I should say that I know it. I learned it on a stair, as a boy, by wanting very much not to be heard by the person at the top. It took me a year to do on purpose and three more to stop doing by accident. It always came at dusk, when the eye is worst and the ear must do the work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:259:"No. It is not a thing that waits to be asked, at that hour, by a tired man, and I was a tired man for some years." He turned his head a very little, not so far as Cael. "It was not spent. Nobody was on the road to see it, and I have the best reason of anyone living to know what it sounds like. I only remark that I heard a person stop being there, and I should prefer that you did not grow to like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:269:*Fifth day. Deployment since the Line: none. At dusk, on an empty road, Shadow got as far as my boots, seal and all. Four strides, both feet, before I had it shut. I didn't ask for it and I didn't use it for anything, so it isn't a deployment. It's what the thing costs to carry. Call it rent. Entered as rent, and Seln heard it, which is right. He would.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:287:They did not talk. He noticed that first, and it was a good hour before he believed it. An old man led, with two mules, and behind him came a boy of fifteen or so with one, and then the woman; and when the old man wanted the boy he did not call back to him, but lifted a hand, and the boy looked. Once the woman clicked her tongue, twice, and the old man slowed. From the middle of the morning until the sun stood in the west Cael heard no word spoken above the creak of the wheels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:311:It was not a blow. The old man had come up behind him without a sound, and he put the boy's arm down as one puts down the lid of a box, and the boy's ears went red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:321:Cael had never heard the word used so, and he knew what it meant before he had finished hearing it. It meant a person from the country of stamps and seals, who had been written down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:331:The claim was that out here nobody on a road declared a Path, at any tier, for any purpose; or lit a fire he could do without, or spoke where a hand would serve; and that everybody showed steel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:339:They were all near enough to hear. Lira had her chin on her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:359:Brom thought that over, and seemed to find it sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:Brom made a sound that he turned into a cough, and Lira looked round at the four of them with her mouth gone very tight at one corner; and then, since nothing else could be done with it, she laughed. It was not a long laugh, and when it was over she wiped her nose on her wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:"He's done," said the ferryman, to the hoof. "I've known it since the thaw. He stops, and I shout, and he goes on because he's honest, and it's a poor trade for the both of us." He let the foot down at last. "What's that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:"What's wrong with him? There's always a thing. Folk don't sell a sound horse at the far end of nowhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:107:They settled the price on an upturned bucket, Brom and the ferryman, and it took a good while, because Brom had counted the old mule's stops. There had been eleven in a single crossing, the day before, and he knew how many carts had stood waiting on the far bank through each one. He set that sum against the gelding's teeth and went on setting it there, patiently, until the ferryman stopped talking about teeth. Cael did not hear the figure, though he heard what went with it, for that had been his own notion: dry room for three boxes in the loft above the drum, where the ferryman's boy slept, for as long as the horse walked underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:153:"Tell me why. I'm not saying no. I want to hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:225:It was not a log. It was a whole tree, a pale one, with its root-plate still on it like a great clenched fist, and it came round the upstream bend riding low and slow, and turning as it came. The ferryman's boy saw it first and shouted, for on a river, it seemed, a person might shout as he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:229:Three things happened in Cael at once, and he had been waiting for them. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, as breath gathers before a shout, for a push at that range would have turned a tree. Storm leaned, and showed him the air between the scow and the trunk as a thing with a grain in it. And Wind remembered a painted floor in his feet, and offered to put him somewhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:279:The woman with the dough had gone back to her pan. She had heard it, though, and so had the man with the harness across his knees, and the three children; and by dark, Cael supposed, the whole meadow would know that five strangers had stood on a boat all day and lost nothing. It was not much to be known for; but it was the first thing that anybody east of a line knew about them at all, and it happened to be true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:281:It bought flour, and salt, and a piece of bacon no bigger than a hand, and Brom told them that it would have kept the gelding for one day and the half of another. Karis wrote the line into the field archive as she had heard it said, and drew her second rule under two of its words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:47:"Delivered at my fence," she said. "And nothing lost that I can see. I'll stand to that." She gave the slip back to the cart. "There's a loft over the barn. The grain goes up in the morning, and you can have what room it leaves. Ladder's sound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:Brom took them up one at a time, on his right shoulder, with his left hand for the ladder. It was the tree on the gravel bar over again, with a twelve-foot drop under it and a floor of flagstones, and Cael, who was drawing water at the well, watched every trip from the side of his eye. On the seventeenth, a rung went. It cracked through under the leading boot with a sound like the wheel had made, and the whole of Brom's weight and a sack of barley came down eighteen inches onto the rung beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:The bowman had been watching her come. He was a lean, weathered man of no particular age, who seemed to live on that platform, and Cael had not yet heard him use his voice. He looked down at Lira standing under him with her hands on her knees. Then he lifted two fingers from the stave of his bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:109:She had made a table of it in her exact hand. At the head stood the word, underlined once, and under the word his five counts in the order he had given them: *keeps station at forty-five yards; makes no sound; follows the buckle with its head; will not cross the gravel fan; answers nothing, having been given nothing.* Beneath those stood the figure, *110 yards*, and after it, in smaller writing, *gate to stone; C.'s count, paced at a yard to the stride; not taped.* She had gone out in the heat of noon and walked the same hundred and ten yards along the inside of the fence, in her own shorter steps, so as to know in her legs how far it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:137:*Subject animal kept station on the party at forty-five yards for something over half a mile, without approach. Halted one hundred and ten yards from the hold's gate and remained until the gate was barred. Withdrew at a walk by the line of its coming. It was waiting upon a signal. The signal was not given. This officer does not know what the signal is.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:*On the forty-third, at dusk, a thing the hold calls a stillhound went beside us for the last half of a mile. Five counts and a distance, given to Karis; she has the name and I have the numbers and neither of us has a reason. Nobody declared anything. Nobody drew. I put all nine down at every stride and they kept getting up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:151:"That's all a hirer wants to hear from me," he said. "Did they come back, and did his goods come with them. I've been asked it every day for thirty years and I've not yet been asked a second question."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:193:"I've forty headings in this book," he said, "and every one's a name. One name. A man, or a woman, and whoever walks with them." He scratched his jaw with the feather end of the pen. "Well. It'll be five names, then. It takes more ink. Say them slow, and I'll write what I hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:199:"Seln," said Seln. He seemed a little surprised by the sound of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:263:She made a sound in her throat, and got up, and walked off up the meadow toward the long eave, between the fires, with her hands in her coat. She was gone as long as it takes to walk to a door and stand in it, and when she came back she sat down in the same place and put her feet to the heat.

