editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:"That's what I wanted you to see. I couldn't have told you." He walked down to her. "Out here a burst is a shout. I don't know who it is that listens, and I don't think Brom's deer were telling us, but every carter on that road believes somebody does. And yours has just shouted without asking you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:83:*Eighteenth day. The nine, as things held. Not what each can do: what it charges me to carry it and leave it alone, which I'm calling rent.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:95:*Shadow. Source protected, and sealed against anyone who might carry the seeing west. It costs from the moment I wake and it drifts at dusk; on the fifth day it got as far as my boots. Two reasons to keep it shut now, where there was one: the seal, and whatever it is a road hears.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:97:*Storm, from Daeva. Gold weight, the heaviest thing I carry, and idle it only sits, so that I tire sooner than the miles account for. If I open it, the read is the air or my own body and the ground, never both, and a lane takes the air. Three lanes so far: after each, everything I have answered a beat late, for ten counts, and ten, and twelve, and came clean at eleven, eleven and thirteen. That is a bill, not a flag. I have not tried a fourth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:99:*Anchor. Unnamed; the hand with no face. Fixed points, and lines between them, and a cage; and one unbinding, done once, on purpose. It sees what is fixed: sure at ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen. I don't know its reach, or its rate, or its price. Ever since the Ostrand road it has asked about every sill and gate and doorway I've gone through, and a town with no wall has a great many doors.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:43:Two men and a girl got up off the bank then, where they had been sitting with their packs between their feet, and came to stand at the woman's back. Cael saw that it was a captain who had spoken, and that her crew had left her to say it alone until it was proved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:323:He had heard their four lines said at the plank, or heard of them: a boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon. And today the stretch, walked at dusk, by the carter's own telling, with a hound pacing them for half the mile; and not one of the five had so much as lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:355:He had no word to give them, for a man did not speak to a bottom line that had cost him nothing. If they were what four lines said, then the board would put them across his road before the snow, and there would be something to say then, and something to price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might, for all she knew, be a declaration too. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:225:So, first: it kept its station, and he checked that three times. The ruts bent a little to the right, toward the swell, and the grey shape gave ground, drifting up the slope until there were forty-five yards between them again. The ruts bent left, and it came down. Whatever it wanted, it wanted it from exactly that far away, like a watcher who has been told his distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:*It tracked the buckle.* That was worth having, and it was all that he had. At forty-five yards the grey head had moved when the buckle moved, and had lost it when the buckle stopped; a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth had drawn no answer that Cael could see. He could not say what sense had found the buckle, and the finding told him nothing of what the thing was waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:241:It would not put a foot on it. It turned, and for the first time it ran, a long flat lope up the slope and round the head of the gully, where the stones began; he lost it against the hill, and found it, sixty yards off, and seventy. Then it came down the far side at the same lope, and dropped to a walk, and it was forty-five yards from the road again before the tailboard was clear of the wet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:He had written that under the inventory an hour ago. The last line of it was his own count and nobody else's: it was for the nine things he carried, and whether he had let one of them out. Since then he had done nothing at all but keep it true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:35:None of that was use. He had spent two years keeping such things down under other people's eyes, and he had let none of them out since a ring of churned turf beside a split thorn, from a caution that he could not yet have given a reason for. But he had begun to see that carrying nine things and not using them was a kind of work, and that it went on all night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:75:He did not drop this load, for Lira was standing under him with the next one. Cael had his own breath stopped, and Anchor had already found the ladder's rail and asked whether it should be held. It had held a doorway once, for twelve seconds, without waiting for his leave, and he answered it quickly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:109:She had made a table of it in her exact hand. At the head stood the word, underlined once, and under the word his five counts in the order he had given them: *keeps station at forty-five yards; makes no sound; follows the buckle with its head; will not cross the gravel fan; answers nothing, having been given nothing.* Beneath those stood the figure, *110 yards*, and after it, in smaller writing, *gate to stone; C.'s count, paced at a yard to the stride; not taped.* She had gone out in the heat of noon and walked the same hundred and ten yards along the inside of the fence, in her own shorter steps, so as to know in her legs how far it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:123:He walked to the white stone. The boy had made it a hundred and ten yards from the gate, at a yard to the stride, and Seln's own longer pace brought him there in a hundred and four, which was the same distance to within a yard or two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:129:It had not circled or lain down, and he could find no place where it had shifted its feet. When it went, it had turned in its own length, and gone back down the valley on the line it had come by, at a walk; he followed that line for two hundred yards with the grass over his head, and the prints lay as evenly as stitches. He cut across to the ruts at three places and paced the gap. It was forty-five yards at each, as nearly as his legs could tell him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:137:*Subject animal kept station on the party at forty-five yards for something over half a mile, without approach. Halted one hundred and ten yards from the hold's gate and remained until the gate was barred. Withdrew at a walk by the line of its coming. It was waiting upon a signal. The signal was not given. This officer does not know what the signal is.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:145:He had now the first hours of eight roads in his head, five out of Lowmarch and three out of this fence, and the manner of an animal's waiting. It was the fullest return he had ever composed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:He considered, walking back up the ruts to the gate, whether any part of it bore upon the day in hand. That was the rule he had made for himself, and he applied it with care, since he distrusted rules that he found convenient. They were inside a fence. They would go down this valley again behind a cart, at noon, with the sun over them. On that day there was nothing in his eight roads that five people needed in order to put one foot before the other, and so he would keep it, as he had kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:481:He had thought he knew what large was. He had seen the continent drawn on a sheet. He had crossed a good part of it, at a wagon's pace, to stand in a hall at Norhold, and he had looked down from a bluff at a river valley every day for two years and thought it a wide thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:109:Cael had been pushed one of those across this counter every week for two years, always in the desk clerk's hand. *Fifth-day, wing three. Supervised, per provision.* This one Seln wrote himself, in the small square hand of his margins, and he wrote one line on it and turned it round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:9:That was not, in itself, a thing to write down, for a man could be late. But the clerk sat with his pencil lifted, and could not go on to his column. In two years the teaching assistant had never once been late. He had never been early either. He had come in by the inner door at the same moment each day, without a sound, so that the clerk had mostly not noticed him arrive, and had only looked up and found the table occupied, as one finds that a clock has been wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:He knew before he had crossed the floor, and it was his ears that told him. For two years a pen had been going at that table from the first bell, a small dry steady sound, and he had added his column against it every morning without once noticing that he did. The room was not making it. He could hear the clock over the counter instead, which he had not heard at that hour since the year before last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:27:On the shelf over the table stood the first manual and the second, and beside them a space. The dust lay a little differently there, in a clean oblong about the length of a forearm, and the clerk looked at it and it told him nothing. He could not have said what had stood in that place. He had sat ten feet from it for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:A man on the house's strength had not come to his work. He was not an enrollee, whose leaving had a form, and he was not faculty, whose leaving had a council. He had been placed with the wing two years ago by a district office's letter, as teaching assistants were, and it was to that office that the house now owed a question. Bracken wrote it before noon, in his upright hand, with one pair of glasses on, and he put no adjective in it, for he had none he could prove. It went down the hill in the First-day bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:*Teaching assistant, Bronze grade, fifteen years' service, absent without notice from first bell of the First-day. Residence room found vacated; no personal effects; bed made. All working files completed and cross-indexed to date. Inner-door key, records-press key, post-room key and wing side-door key surrendered on the copying-table blotter. Floor schedule posted one week forward. No note left, no leave applied for, no address of forwarding held by this house. The house records no disciplinary matter, no irregularity of any kind in the two years of this posting, and no view as to cause. Query submitted to the placing district office per code; direction requested.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:It was the right question, the one any careful officer asks when a quiet man goes. Nobody at that desk thought he had taken anything. He had sat at a copying table, and held four keys, and laid all four out in a row. But for two years he had been closer to one subject than anybody else in the service, and before that he had been close to sixty-two others, and he had watched, and written what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:103:The boy came to the counter at the third bell with a card he had no need to bring. Seln took it and gave him a line on a floor sheet, and put the sheet where the bundles were going. He thought the boy had seen the ash. He thought, too, that the boy had understood it, and would write none of it down, and two years ago he could not have relied on that. It was a strange thing to have taught somebody, and stranger to be glad of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:107:He held it a little longer than the rest. It was two years of true weeks. *Subject attended. Subject's figure as entered by the house.* He had been proud of the others once, and there was a commendation for them in a drawer. He was not proud of this one. He was something else, and he did not have the word, and the catechism had never supplied it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:129:For two years he had fetched the house's pouch across that bridge, at the end of a day, with the same satchel on the same shoulder. He nodded to the ward's lantern and was nodded to, and went over the middle arch above the brown water, and nobody would think of it again. The two coats were at the bluff road's foot and the two at the landing, where they had been set to stand, and they had not been set to watch a clerk go for the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:179:There was a crossroads beyond it. A lane ran north to a farm and another south toward the river, and in the angle of them lay a piece of rough common with a thorn brake along one side, and a stream that went under the south lane through a pipe of fired clay. He had passed the place twice in two years, on the house's errands, and had marked it each time for no reason he would have admitted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:91:Cael watched him. He had seen that face across a counter for two years and had never once seen it lose, and it was losing now, to three people at a fire; and he thought that somewhere under the mildness the man was more pleased than he had been since a birthday.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:177:They had pooled it in a parlour in Ostrand, and a cart and an old horse had come out of it, and four nights' hay. Cael had put in what Hesk had sent him over two years, a little at a time, in letters, and what he had not spent, and he had thought it a fair sum until he saw the others'. Lira's was small. Brom's was a good deal, and he had said only that the estate paid a son's allowance whether the son answered his letters or not. Karis had the price of some books she had sold to the lake house. Seln's had come later, at the crossroads fire, out of the post satchel in a flat leather wallet, and nobody had remarked on it then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:219:The sixth was the one that stayed shut. It had been sealed for two years and more, and the seal held tonight exactly as it had held on the hill. Only one thing in that line had changed. The man it came from had been told, and he sat across the fire with his cup in his hands and an empty satchel at his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:47:- The Power Log in Hesk's volume (the current book since B5 T26: the year's ledger, the six guesses, the B4 minute and the costed rule are in it); the old volume, full — its last line written on the last ferry (B5 ch60:191) — carrying Hesk's reply (verbatim ch60:163) and the other letters behind its front board (ch60:165); the observation notebook (Halcenvane section, two years; eleven Paths charted from the Crown yard's east tier). Cael's T26 letters to Hesk and Vell went by Bracken's pouch (ch60:39–49); no reply from Vell is on B5's page [B5-reconciled 2026-10-06: audit §F D9]. Hesk's satchel does **not** exist yet (eighteenth-birthday gift, M5).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:166:- **Seln:** knows the instrument came (the green form; the lamp in the wing). He has never been told why Shadow was sealed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:416:- **The inventory:** seven confirmed (Shadow sealed among them; Storm the seventh) and the still place uncounted. Before Norhold there were six, with five on the registry's record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:456:- Seln has not been told why Shadow is sealed (B6 Ch15). No falsification / [UNBOUND] / maker / Tide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:471:- **Seln:** he produced the sheaf (and denies it); he has watched one sitting from the sightline seat; he reads three grades of officer. He has still never been told why Shadow was sealed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:553:**Coordinator correction (2026-10-10).** Seln learns why Shadow was sealed in SOURCE Ch15 (The Cache), which is the edition's MOVEMENT 6 (BOOK_MAP §3 item 15, §6, §9) — not in M3. Until M6 he does not know a sealed thing exists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:562:- SELN is NOT told why Shadow was sealed — that is source Ch15, the edition's M6 (coordinator correction 2026-10-10). Until M6 he does not know a sealed thing exists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:586:- **Seln:** has still **not** been told why Shadow is sealed (see owner flag 1). He knows what he has always known; he has watched Cael learn the lodge book and the post sort.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:639:- (a) **Seln is not told why Shadow was sealed** (owner flag 1).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:704:- **Seln:** has still **not** been told why Shadow was sealed and does not know a sealed thing exists (his window touches only the two slips, the coals, the season's figures and Norhold). The maintenance records' content is not stated anywhere. He knows the clerk's timing at the confluence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:794:- Seln never learns why Shadow was sealed in M5 and never sees the reach (under the wagon, the linchpin). His ch29 old press "a little above where it should have sat… put there by somebody who could reach" is a contentless plant for M6.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:803:**Calendar.** No season or month names; feet and yards; Halcenvane weekdays. Day 77 Seventh-day: Seln at Bracken's counter; two favours; the drop; rain from the morning. Day 78 First-day: the cards read in the annex. Day 79 Second-day: the reach priced. Day 80 Third-day: the mould-room answer; Havel given the letter. Day 81 Fourth-day: the western answer; the grades; the catechism; the case carried to Cael at dusk; the cipher (five arrivals on day 85); the null report; the bridge chained at the third ring after the fourth bell, the town ferry tied up at noon; Havel's sixth entry that night. **Day 82 Fifth-day: the upper ferry; the cut (mid-morning); the reach and the unbinding; the fifth sitting (documentary) at the hour after noon; home over the bridge at dusk (water under the second ring).** Day 83 Sixth-day: schedule posted a day early (the five moved to day 88); fixity; Storm resettles (40 min; 15 min); the doorway (twelve seconds); Rooke. Day 84 Seventh-day: the idle state found by naming at dawn; Karis's curve; the district seat's answer. **Day 87 Third-day: the eleventh of Sowing — eighteen.** Day 88 Fourth-day: the annex (tomorrow at close). Brom's panel and the ruling share the year's last Fifth-day. The *fled* default would enter forty days after day 75.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:806:- **Cael:** **eighteen** from day 87. Left forearm skinned wrist to elbow on the camber (dressed and bound; itching by day 87); **right ribs, two cracked, not through** (eight days from day 82, to ~day 90). Right shoulder's word at the fourth push. Pressure spent at fighting load (four pushes); the spark once; one lane (ten counts). Excused from wing three (ribs) — no band bout this week. Anchor integrated to the **idle state by naming** (about two days); still leans toward thresholds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:815:- **Seln:** the paper traced (the second run; the fourteenth lot above the ruled line, a mark for a word); the four grades; has decided to open the case; **has not been told why Shadow was sealed and does not know a sealed thing exists**; **did not see the reach or the unbinding** (he was under the wagon in the gully; "I heard it"); knows the team broke off and the boy has ribs. **Does not know the mechanism.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:833:- **Deployments:** Anchor — **one deliberate use (the unbinding)**; **one uncommanded fixing (the doorway, twelve seconds, day 83)**. No other Anchor use; no hold. Shadow offered once in E1, refused; never used. In the fight: the Wind once, the push ×4 at fighting load, the read (contact; and given back), one lane, the spark once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:904:- SELN'S SEAL TERMS (as amended by the audit for B7's witness/exposure logic): while Shadow's use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west, it stays sealed; the debt is closed; the seal stays on Seln's new terms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:918:- **Cael:** ribs called mended about day 90 (a week before day 98); forearm healing, no dressing by the honest floor. Anchor: perception only on day 92; **nothing laid, nothing fixed**; one near-fixing stopped by naming at twelve feet (Brom counted "One"); a dull ache behind the eyes after. Shadow: sealed, rent from waking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:943:- **Deployments: none.** Anchor perception only (no point laid; the twelve-foot near-fixing stopped by naming). Shadow sealed. No Storm lane, no burst, no read on a floor in M6.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1033:- Seln: the mechanism not said to him again — the "twice more" count unchanged; his five beans. Nobody leaves, resigns or votes; Anchor idle; Shadow sealed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1043:- **Cael:** unhurt. The night spar's bills (day 129): right hip crest to knee from the sixth burst on boards; shoulder, three pushes at load; one lane, ten counts; a naming mid-fight; behind the eyes "till morning". Day 151: a knee wrenched on purpose, as priced. Anchor idle by naming (asks at a gateway in the dark, at Lira's planted feet, at little else by the last month). Shadow sealed on Seln's terms; one drift on the night road, held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1076:- **Deployments:** public — the band in wing three (day 151: one small burst, the give shut, the read; 23⅔; never past the Halcenvane ceiling). Private, two people, a closed floor (day 129) — Wind to seven on boards, the push at fighting load ×3, the give (three blows), one spark, one lane (ten counts), the read at depth. **Anchor: none** (idle; two namings). **Shadow: none** (one drift held). The honest floor once in the term, perception only (ten feet, faint at twelve).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1185:- **Anchor is not used in the yard; Shadow stays sealed.** The public sees Wind to ten, the fighting-load push, two Storm lanes, the give, one spark, and the deep read. The managed band is over. Book 7 must still open with “Deployment since the Line: none” (B6 `BOOK_MAP.md:74–79,533–539`; `packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:15–17,27`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1210:- **Cael:** left forearm bound, five lines across it wrist to elbow (the touch in the first exchange and four more on the guard), nothing broken, "the colour of a plum by morning" and so for a fortnight (the healer). Right hip, crest to knee: ten bursts on the oak, four past the six. Right shoulder: six pushes at load, one past Norhold's five. Two lanes, ten counts and ten. Behind the eyes. One spark. No flag (no wrong thing answered). Anchor idle by naming; not used. Shadow sealed; it offered nothing. Blade-adjacent: integration partial; first pull at the yard gate's lintel, named and quieted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1328:- **Progression.** Nine confirmed fragments; the Tide-adjacent anomaly is uncounted and "came from nobody". Anchor idle, undeployed. Shadow sealed (witness-can-carry-it-west logic), not deployed since the Line. The sixth entry stays sealed and the eighth unnamed in the inventory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1348:**Knowledge, progression and reserved truths — PASS.** Nine fragments are confirmed; the Tide-adjacent anomaly remains uncounted. Anchor is idle and undeployed; Shadow is sealed under the witness-can-carry-it-west logic and has not been deployed since the Line. Six people know the falsification/cache history, but only the five travelers carry the integration mechanism into Book 7. The text does not name the Architect, Quieting or the reserved Path term. The old sigil-stone/station relationship remains an observation without maker, purpose or age disclosure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1365:- **Cael:** eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted Tide-adjacent anomaly** (*Still open. Still real. Patience.*). **Deployment since the Line: none.** The forearm bruise is a week old and about a week from gone; no open injury. Shadow sealed on Seln's terms; it costs from waking and drifts at dusk; its term is nearly met out here and he has left it shut. Hesk's book has about a third of its leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:182:At the far end of the stand, Gault had not moved from beside his case. The magister was looking across the oak at him, steadily, as he had looked across a trestle every week for two years with three cards and a glass. He had signed a figure for every one of those weeks, and every figure had been earned on the house's floors, to the decimal, exactly as it was written; and he had just been shown what they had been figures of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:224:"I've trained on this hill with him for two years," she said. "I've watched every wing-three card there was. I never saw one bit of that. Not one." She wiped her nose on her wrist. "Where's he been *keeping* it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:185:He drew a single small leaf from under the daybook and turned it round. It was Gault's hand, the panel-note hand, as square as a ruled table, which Cael had seen once a week for two years at the foot of three cards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:There was a bed, and a press, and a table, and a sill, and he had lived among them for two years and the better part of a third. Seln had given the rule in an orchard: forty pounds for a day, and thirty for thirty days. Cael's came to less than thirty. He folded two shirts and the spare coat, and rolled the strapping the healer had left him, and put Hesk's clock into a stocking; it was a quarter of an hour behind the bell, and losing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:241:The satchel took the rest, as it had been cut to. Hesk's book went into its pocket, and behind it the old volume, full to its last leaf, with the letters under its front board, and beside them the observation notebook, which held two years of the Crown yard's east tier. He did not own a thing he would grieve to lose that was not now on that strap.

