editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:329:"It wants the line," said Cael. "It thinks it's a doorway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:331:"Two points and a line between them," said Karis. "That's all a doorway is, to it, isn't it? Jamb and jamb and the space between." She wrote it. "Name it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:349:The chalk line at twelve feet lay on the boards between its two small fires, and the new thing, which had been faint about it, was suddenly not faint. It leaned. It leaned the way it had leaned in his own doorway with Lira coming through it on her crutch, when he had been halfway through saying *no*. Something in him began to go out of him toward the line, quick and quiet as a breath, to set a point at one end of it and a point at the other and tell the space between them to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:He walked. It was the old walk, at every angle, with the lath low, but he was not walking to learn weight now. He was laying lines. He turned his wrist and there was one across the middle of the oak, level, a bar across a doorway, and he turned it again and there was a second, slantwise, from the rim wall to the first. He could not hold more than two at once; Cael saw that, and saw that he did not need to. He was building a corner out of two lines and four feet of sunk stone wall.
