editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:219:He gave her the whole of his attention before he answered. It was the look he had given the room from the doorway, slow and level and entire, the look of a man who has been sent to evaluate something and means to do it fairly even when the something is a girl with a turned ankle asking him a straight question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:271:He thought of the room in Ostrand, though he had told himself on the post road that he would not. Four young people and a man at a window. The man had stood a little to one side of the glass, where the frame hid him from the lane, the way the field service teaches its people to stand, and had copied forty orders in a round level hand that never once let itself be hurried. The Archmarshal had known what he was from the doorway. He had known, reading, roughly what the copying must have cost and how many years it had sat somewhere before it came to that table. He did not know the man's name, and he had been careful all the way home not to wonder it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:291:He had a file under his arm, a different one, and a question about a guild hall's frame that took a minute to ask and a minute to answer. When it was answered he did not go. He stood in the doorway with the file against his chest, nervous and good, and looked past the Archmarshal at the tray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:311:At the sixth bell his clerk stood in the doorway with the day's wallet against his chest, looking not at the desk but past it, at the window, where the evening had come in grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:189:In the doorway the officer stood and watched them. He was pleased. Anybody could have seen it. He had done a thing he had done ten thousand times, and done it properly, and a child was happy, and he liked his work. He looked at his board, and turned it over, and went in to see who was next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:121:He told it the way he had written it, standing against a wall under a lamp in the covered walk with the notebook held to the stone at the third hour. The archive at Halcenvane on a bad night two years ago, near enough, in the first year he was on the hill. The fire-watch gone the long way round. A man with a bag at the top of the upper flight, in the doorway, with his gloved hand already going into a press he had no business opening. And on the landing above him, in the light, the wing's assistant. He was reading the man a form, one plain line after another, about copying and paper and what a magistrate counted in years, until the man's hand came back out of the press with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:129:"I knew there was somebody in the corner on the second night, and I knew who on the third." Seln's voice had not changed at all. "I did not look down the flight. A man in a doorway with a bag looks where the officer looks, so I did not look."
